xinput, winebus: no buttons is a legal device, and the haptics stub has none.

mgvf-0029 asked a question with a registry value and the answer came back the
same day: with the haptics device's never-pressed button gone, Ghost of Tsushima
keeps L3 held. With it there, it does not. So the button was the cost, the
candidate written into mgvf-0029's header was right, and what is left is to make
the better packet the one everybody gets.

TWO HALVES, AND THE ORDER MATTERS.

FIRST, xinput stops allocating from a count it has not looked at:

    if (!(button_caps = malloc(sizeof(*button_caps) * ...NumberInputButtonCaps))) return FALSE;

A device with no buttons at all is legal HID. malloc(0) is allowed to return
NULL, and on that line NULL meant the whole device was refused -- so a
controller offering nothing but motors would have been dropped for having no
buttons to count, which is the one thing it was never going to have. The block
is skipped when the count is zero and nothing else changes; `button_count` stays
0 and the "Too few buttons, continuing anyway" warning below already handles
that case, which is how we know the rest of the function was always ready for
it.

THEN the stub's default turns over. The button existed only to keep that
allocation away from zero, and it was always OUR xinput that would have hit it:
since mgvf-0028 this device is published under the xinput interface and no
other, so the only code that opens it is code this set ships. With the hazard
fixed at its own end there is nothing left on the other side of the trade.

WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not a claim about why the game behaves that way. A
device reporting every button released on every poll is the shape of a held
button being cleared, and that is still the reading rather than a measurement of
the game's code. What is measured is the outcome: L3 holds without the button
and does not hold with it, on one title, with one option between the two runs.

XInputRumbleStubButton 1 puts the old device back, for a client that turns out
to want a button after all.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
